Phase 2. Design and implement based on the Gherkin specification.

---

## Context-dependent behaviour

| Context | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| First cycle | Full design and implementation |
| Later cycles | Reflect the design of affected parts and fix the implementation |

---

## Implementation basis

Use the GWT (Gherkin) of approved scenarios as the basis for functional behaviour.
Do not arbitrarily add behaviour not included in the Gherkin.
Do not reference, copy, adapt, or reuse Phase 1 prototype code as a basis for
Phase 2 implementation. Carry approved behaviour forward through the approved
scenarios' GWT specifications. Develop the production code in `implementation/`,
separately from the exploration code in `prototypes/`.

This restriction applies to exploration code, including prototypes made in later
cycles. Later cycles may modify the existing production implementation according
to the updated approved GWT; they do not require rebuilding it from scratch.

---

## Internal information system

Accumulate the following information during implementation. Keep the format
lightweight, but at a level that enables impact analysis in later cycles.

### Design-decision record
What was decided, why, and which alternatives were dropped.
Notes for understanding the structure after context is reset.

### Scenario-code mapping
Which scenario (SCN-NNN) corresponds to which code.
The basis for judging the impact scope on a change request.

### Tests
Create tests together with the implementation.
Include both Gherkin-based acceptance tests and internal unit/integration tests.

---

## Sufficiency criteria

The internal information system is sufficient when it meets both of:
- A level that enables impact analysis in later cycles
- A level from which, if the user requests traditional artifacts (design
  documents, test plans, etc.), they can be generated and provided

---

## Transition to Phase 3

When implementation and test creation are complete, switch to Phase 3
(verification).
# Non-functional backlog

- The web app should not be broken when used from a phone, although it is primarily intended for a computer browser. A separate mobile app is not required.
- The client prefers the link paste box to remain visible on the collection page, avoiding an extra click when saving links repeatedly. The client described this as a small style choice rather than a major concern.
- The client prefers tag entry to stay inline on the bookmark because it feels quicker and lighter for frequent tagging; a separate dialog feels like unnecessary ceremony. The client described this as a minor preference.
- The client prefers tags to remain visibly laid out above the collection rather than hidden in a dropdown, both for faster selection and as a reminder of which tags exist. The client described this as a minor preference.
- The client mildly prefers a checkbox for the read-later flag because its checked or unchecked state is easy to read at a glance.
- The client prefers the focused read-later view to use a plainly labeled “Mark as read” action for clearing an item, because unchecking a box can feel like merely undoing a mistake. This works alongside the checkbox used to show and set flag state in the full collection; the client described the distinction as an ideal rather than a strong requirement.
- The client prefers direct bold and list controls for note formatting rather than typed formatting symbols. A live preview before saving is reassuring and should ideally accompany those controls; the client described this as a mild preference.
- The client mildly prefers a focused edit window for changing the bookmark address, title, and description together because it feels tidier and less cramped than editing in place.
- The client mildly prefers email-and-password sign-in over a delivered code because this frequently used app should not require waiting for and copying a code each time.
- The client mildly prefers loading more bookmarks into one continuous list rather than numbered pages because continuous scrolling better preserves their sense of place during casual browsing. An overall count should still communicate collection size.
# Later-cycle backlog

Requests recorded after the Cycle 1 scenario baseline was approved. These are not part of Cycle 1 implementation or acceptance.

## Sorting the collection

> Putting the list in an order I choose. We just talked about big collections loading in batches, but I never got to say how they're sorted. I'd want to flip between, say, newest first and oldest, or sort by title alphabetically — whatever makes sense for what I'm looking for at the moment.

Explore client-selected collection ordering, including newest first, oldest first, and alphabetical title order.

## Bulk actions

> Doing something to several bookmarks at once. So far every action — tagging, read-later, setting aside, deleting — I've done one bookmark at a time. Once I've got a big pile, I'd want to select a bunch of them (or everything currently matching my search or tag) and, say, tag them all or set them all aside in one go, instead of clicking through them one by one.

Explore selecting several bookmarks or all bookmarks matching active search/filter criteria, then applying actions such as tagging or setting aside in one operation.

## Advanced search expressions

> A couple of smarter search moves. The word-and-tag search is great, but sometimes I'd want to search for an *exact phrase* in quotes, or say "this but not that" — combine words with something like "and," "or," "not" to narrow in. Just more precise finding when the simple search casts too wide a net.

Explore exact-phrase matching and explicit AND, OR, and NOT combinations beyond the approved simple case-insensitive word-and-tag search.
